  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Due to its small size, Oxygen exhibits high electronegativity, a high charge/ radius ratio, and no d-orbital oxygen shows anomalous behaviour.</p><p>Oxygen exists as a diatomic molecule as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> comprising a double bond whereas all the other elements of group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> exist as polyatomic molecules.</p><p>Oxygen is paramagnetic and exists in a gaseous state at room temperature, which is completely opposite of the other Group <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn></math> elements.</p><p>The small size and high electronegativity of Oxygen enable it in the formation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pπ</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>pπ</mi></math> multiple bonds.</p>",
    "image": null
  },
